<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transkei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1976">1976</a>–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Transkei was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">Bantustan</a>, or homeland, for Xhosa-speaking people in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, with its capital at Umtata (Mthatha). It received self-government in 1963 and in 1976 became the first homeland declared independent under apartheid, a status no other country recognised. It was reincorporated into South Africa in 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/transkei.jpg" alt="Transkei" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Makaristos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Transkei.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Grand apartheid assigned Africans to ten ethnic ‘homelands’, four of which, <b>Transkei</b> (1976), Bophuthatswana (1977), Venda (1979) and Ciskei (1981), were declared independent, although no other country recognised them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
